1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,418
Your graphics card gets about sixteen milliseconds to draw each frame at sixty frames per second.

2
00:00:06,698 --> 00:00:08,882
Miss it, and the frame shows up late.

3
00:00:09,632 --> 00:00:12,437
That is just one second divided by sixty.

4
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:17,676
At a hundred and forty four hertz, the budget shrinks to under seven milliseconds.

5
00:00:18,426 --> 00:00:23,836
Inside that window, the CPU decides what's on screen and sends draw calls.

6
00:00:24,116 --> 00:00:30,569
Then the GPU moves every vertex, finds which pixels each triangle covers, and colors them.

7
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:35,762
At full HD, that's over two million pixels, every single frame.

8
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:39,078
Only then is the finished picture sent to your screen.

9
00:00:39,828 --> 00:00:41,533
And averages hide misses.

10
00:00:41,813 --> 00:00:47,640
One frame that takes forty milliseconds feels like a hitch, even when the average looks fine.

11
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:53,613
Watch one frame make the whole trip in our video, How a GPU Actually Works.

